Bug: browsing from inside a macro body needs a batch -create

Cold, with no snapshot, this answers "No reference" where the same navigation
works once -create has run. Two causes were found (2026-09-18); the first is
fixed, the second is what still fails here.

1. FIXED in 3a67dc91. Entry refresh Pass 3 never parsed the compilation units
   that include the request file when the request file is a header, so nothing
   that expands the macro was ever parsed. A -create snapshot hid it, because it
   carries an identity include reference (the header "defines" itself) that made
   Pass 3's test pass by accident.

2. OPEN. findMacroExpansionFile (src/cxref.c) looks for a
   UsageMacroBaseFileUsage reference to pick a CU that expands the macro, and
   expandMacroCall records one only in XrefMode (src/yylex.c, "if (options.mode
   == XrefMode) addMacroBaseUsageRef(...)"). XrefMode is what -create runs, so a
   server-only session never records it.

Removing that condition makes this test pass - verified 2026-09-18, the answer
becomes the <display-or-update-browser> in expected. The cost is that every
macro then gets a reference at line 0, column 0 in the snapshot, which shows up
in the tests that dump the database: 23 of them fail, all in the collation and
token-pasting family (test_token_pasting*, test_collate_*, test_parsing_c23,
test_parsing_gnuisms, test_parsing_stringification, test_parsing_va_args_empty,
test_parse_expand_stdc_version, test_define_undefine_define).

So the fix is one line plus a decision:
- accept the reference in the dumps and add the "@file:0:0" line to those 23
  expected files, in the same commit; or
- keep it out of the snapshot or out of the dump (utils/cxref_reader), and say
  why a reference the server needs in memory should not be persisted.

Coordinate before doing it: that test family is where the other machine has been
working (four collation commits on 2026-09-18).
